WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.051 --> 00:00:06.480
Speaker 1: Yesterday, President Joe Biden spoke to&nbsp;
the nation again about the situation in Russia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.640
Ukraine, with Vladimir Putin invading Ukraine on&nbsp;
the premise that, Hey, we've got to keep the peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.000
in these independent regions that I've recognized&nbsp;
as independent, even though they're in another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.720
country. And we already talked yesterday&nbsp;
about why that entire framing doesn't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.000
any sense. It violates a number of different&nbsp;
international protocols. But the latest is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.280
well, what is Joe Biden going to do? And Joe&nbsp;
Biden is in a politically difficult situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.520
because no matter what he does, the right is&nbsp;
going to criticize him. The right is criticizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:46.000
Joe Biden. So let let's go back first. The right&nbsp;
attacked Biden when it came to Russia, saying, We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.040
should want to be friends with Russia. We should.&nbsp;
We don't want conflict with Russia. We don't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.800
war with Russia. Biden, OK. But insisting that&nbsp;
Biden would be too harsh on Russia, whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.200
Trump was more friendly and open? Yeah, we know.&nbsp;
Then very quickly, the right shifted to saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.840
Trump would have been would have been harsher&nbsp;
with Russia. So Putin never would have tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:14.800
this if Trump was still president. Biden is being&nbsp;
too weak with Russia. But then at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.680
they're sort of defending what Vladimir&nbsp;
Putin is doing, saying, Yeah, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.840
Putin seems to be going to places where they&nbsp;
want him in. Ukraine will get to that later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.840
and kind of defending what Vladimir Putin is&nbsp;
doing. And at the same time, they are saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.960
well, the sanctions that Biden is putting on&nbsp;
Russia are not nearly strict enough. So they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.440
all sides of this. I want to just speak clearly&nbsp;
to you. Joe Biden has put in place some sanctions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.680
and we're going to talk about those here as&nbsp;
Biden yesterday, making it clear that this is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.360
invasion by Vladimir Putin. Take a look.
Speaker 2: Last few months, we've coordinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.920
closely with our NATO allies and partners&nbsp;
in Europe and around the world to prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.520
that response. We've said all along and&nbsp;
I've told Putin to his face some a month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.840
more than a month ago, that we would act together.&nbsp;
And the moment Russia moved against Ukraine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.400
Russia is now undeniably moved against&nbsp;
Ukraine by declaring these independent states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:21.120
So today I'm announcing the first tranche of&nbsp;
sanctions to impose cost on Russia in response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.640
to their actions yesterday. These have been&nbsp;
closely coordinated with our allies and partners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.560
and will continue to escalate sanctions of Russia&nbsp;
as. We're implementing full blocking sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:40.640
on two large Russian financial institutions,&nbsp;
VTB and their military bank. We're implementing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.960
comprehensive sanctions on Russian sovereign debt.&nbsp;
That means we've cut off Russia's government from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:54.160
Western financing. It can no longer raise money&nbsp;
from the West and cannot trade in its new debt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:00.800
our markets or European markets, either starting&nbsp;
tomorrow and continuing in the days ahead. We'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.480
also impose sanctions on Russia's elites and their&nbsp;
family members they share in the corrupt gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.920
that the Kremlin policies and should share in the&nbsp;
pain as well. And because of Russia's actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.800
we work with Germany to ensure Nord Stream two&nbsp;
will not, as I promised, will not move forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.440
as Russia contemplates its next move. We have&nbsp;
our next moves prepared as well. Russia will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.320
pay an even steeper price if it continues its&nbsp;
aggression, including additional sanctions.&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:37.120
Speaker 1: OK, and then Joe Biden going on to say&nbsp;
that even what he has done so far is not the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.840
scope of what he is willing to do. Take a look.
Speaker 2: This is the beginning of a Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.880
invasion of Ukraine, as he indicated and asked&nbsp;
permission to be able to do from his Duma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.960
So let's begin to. So I'm going to&nbsp;
begin to impose sanctions in response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.240
far beyond the steps we and our allies&nbsp;
and partners implemented in 2014.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.400
And if Russia goes further with this invasion, we&nbsp;
stand prepared to go further, as with sanctions.&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.400
Speaker 1: So the big discussion, the serious&nbsp;
discussion, you know, all these right wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.360
gutter people that are just going to criticize&nbsp;
Biden, no matter what he does, I'm not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.600
interested in engaging with them. Let's let's&nbsp;
engage with a serious criticism, which is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:26.800
are these sanctions going far enough? And&nbsp;
despite repeating that the U.S. won't send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:32.400
troops to Ukraine, is the U.S. basically fighting&nbsp;
this as a proxy war? So let's take these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.240
questions one by one. First of all, are the&nbsp;
sanctions put in place by the United States?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.680
That strict? And the answer is sorta, but&nbsp;
not really. One of the real concerns about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:52.720
the very first set of sanctions was you're really&nbsp;
just sanctioning the regions that Putin invaded.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:59.920
You're not really hitting Russians and Russia&nbsp;
directly now with the sanctioning of Russian banks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:06.400
and more Russian billionaires and their families&nbsp;
and their assets and ability to move assets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:14.640
you are more directly hitting Russia proper.&nbsp;
But you could go a lot further. Why isn't Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.520
doing it? Well, you'd have to ask him why&nbsp;
he's not doing it, but it makes sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.160
you've got to keep something off the you've&nbsp;
got to keep something on the table. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.480
in case Putin goes further because there&nbsp;
is speculation at this point, and so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.000
most of the speculation has been borne out to be&nbsp;
accurate in terms of what Putin has done so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.440
There's speculation that Putin's ambitions are&nbsp;
greater than the Donetsk and Luhansk regions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:48.800
so far he has sent troops to. If Putin wants to&nbsp;
push further into Ukraine up to and including the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.720
Ukrainian capital Kiev, and wants to try to effect&nbsp;
regime change installing a puppet government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:01.120
who knows what Putin's ambitions are, you need to&nbsp;
leave something more on the table geopolitically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.080
and from a foreign relations standpoint.&nbsp;
It makes sense you don't do everything yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:12.720
because you suspect Putin has not done everything&nbsp;
yet. So we are getting to harsher sanctions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:20.000
And it and it is logical to not go fully out&nbsp;
because you've got to be able to bring down more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.320
if and when Vladimir Putin pushes further&nbsp;
into Ukraine. Now on the proxy war question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:31.360
I think we just have to accept that this time that&nbsp;
the U.S. is waging a proxy war against Russia now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:38.080
sometimes by proxy war, we mean both parties are&nbsp;
using proxies. In this case, it is Russia pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:47.120
into Ukraine and the U.S. using Naito sanctions&nbsp;
and arming of Ukraine and supporting Ukraine as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:53.520
a proxy to fighting Russia directly. And proxy&nbsp;
wars are not objectively good or bad, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.640
have to recognize that while Joe Biden has said&nbsp;
we're not going to put boots on the ground, we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:05.440
not dropping, we're not using drones. And at least&nbsp;
for the time being, we're not doing cyber attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:13.840
on Russia. We are engaged in a sort of war here&nbsp;
and that is not an endorsement or a criticism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:19.840
but it is a reality and that is something that&nbsp;
we're going to discuss more on tomorrow's program

